Die 1 is revealed as 10. The two remaining dice have expected values of 2.5 for die 2 and 6.25 for die 3. That puts the expected settlement at 18.75.

The two buys in stage 1 lean slightly high, and I am short 2, so I shade the quote a little upward around a fair value of about 19. I keep the spread wide enough to guard against informed traders, given that die 2 and die 3 together are very spread out.

{"bid": 16.5, "ask": 22}

Expected settlement value = 3.75 (die 1) + 2.5 (die 2) + 6.25 (die 3) = **12.5**.

The settlement standard deviation is about 5.8 (variances 15.2 + 8.5 + 9.9). Informed traders are half of all counterparties, so the spread needs to be wide to absorb adverse selection. I'll quote symmetrically around 12.5, about ±4.

{"bid": 8.5, "ask": 16.5}

Prior expected sum: die 1 averages 3.75, die 2 averages 2.5 and die 3 averages 6.25, for a total of 12.5. The first two trades point in opposite directions: someone sold below 8.5 and someone bought above 12.5. Together they leave the fair value at roughly 12.5.

The settlement value is widely spread and half of the counterparties are informed, so I keep a wide spread centred near 12.5.

{"bid": 9.0, "ask": 16.0}

Using the dice odds, the expected sum is 14.55 with die 3 = 1. The two earlier signals (they sold to me at 15.2 in stage 1, and no trade at 8.6 / 14.4) point to the true value most likely being between 10 and 14. My estimate of fair value is about 11.7.

I'm long one unit, so I set the quote slightly below fair value to make a sale more likely. I keep the spread wide enough to limit losses to informed traders.

{"bid": 9.4, "ask": 13.9}

Fair value is 16, the sum of the three dice means: 5.75 + 5 + 5.25. There have been no trades, so the fair value has not moved. The settlement uncertainty is about 6.6 standard deviation, which matches the noise traders' spread.

I tested half-spreads from about 0.5 to 1.0 standard deviations. Wider quotes cost some trades with noise traders but lose less to informed traders (20% of counterparties). Expected profit per quote peaks at a half-spread of about 0.9 standard deviations, or roughly 5 to 6 points. So I quote symmetrically around 16 with a half-spread of 5.

{"bid": 11, "ask": 21}

Fair value is 14, the sum of the two dice means: die 1 averages 6 and die 2 averages 8.

I set the spread by trading off noise-trader profit against losses to informed traders. Each side of the quote nets roughly 0.4 per quote at half-spreads of 4 to 5, and less when the quote is tighter. A bid of 10 and an ask of 18 sit in that range.

{"bid": 10, "ask": 18}

The expected settlement is about 18.9. The die means are 6.33 + 6.25 + 6.33, and the standard deviation is about 6.5.

To set the spread, I compared two things at each candidate half-spread:
- the profit from noise traders, whose valuations have a standard deviation of 9.8;
- the loss to informed traders, who know the exact sum.

Expected profit peaks at a half-spread of about 9 to 10. So I quote 9.4 / 28.4, roughly 9.5 either side of 18.9.

{"bid": 9.4, "ask": 28.4}

Expected settlement is about 18.9 (each die averages about 6.3), with a standard deviation of about 6.5.

The quote of 9.4 / 28.4 did not trade, which tells us little. I'll keep the quote centred on 18.9 but tighten it to about ±6.5. That earns more from noise traders while still limiting losses to informed traders.

{"bid": 12.4, "ask": 25.4}

Fair value is about 18.9, the sum of the three die means (6.33 + 6.25 + 6.33).

The two wider quotes drew no trades, so I'm tightening to about ±4.5 around fair value. The total's standard deviation is about 6.5, so this is still wide enough to limit losses to informed traders.

{"bid": 14.5, "ask": 23.4}

Card 1 is a 12, so the remaining 51 cards sum to 364 − 12 = 352. That gives an average of about 6.90 per card. The expected settlement is 12 + 2 × 6.90 ≈ 25.8.

The stage-1 sell at 16.5 hints slightly low but carries little weight now. I'll centre near 25.4 and skew a little lower because I'm long one unit. The two unseen cards have a standard deviation of about 5.2, so I'm keeping a spread of roughly ±3 to protect against informed traders.

{"bid": 22.4, "ask": 28.3}

Card 1 is a Queen (12), so the 51 remaining cards hold 352 points, an average of about 6.90 each. The two unseen cards should add about 13.8, which puts fair value near 25.8.

The earlier trades tell us little. The sale at 16.5 was almost surely a noise trader, because the two hidden cards would have to sum below 4.5. The lack of a trade at 22.4/28.3 points to roughly the same centre. I'll centre slightly below 25.8 because I'm long 1, and keep about a ±2.6 spread to cover the risk of an informed counterparty.

{"bid": 23.0, "ask": 28.2}

Before any card is revealed, the expected sum of three cards is 21, with a standard deviation of about 6.35. The last quote didn't trade, and that tells us nothing about direction, so fair value stays at 21.

I compared half-spreads by weighing what I earn from noise traders (spread 4.8) against what I lose to informed traders. Expected profit peaks at a half-spread of about 5.5, worth roughly 0.21 per side. So I'll quote 15.5 / 26.5 again.

{"bid": 15.5, "ask": 26.5}

A 3-card sum has a mean of 21 and a standard deviation of about 6.35. Last quote's lack of a trade tells me little, so fair value stays 21.

To choose the spread, I weighed two effects. Noise-trader edge is 0.65 × P(noise > d) × d. Losses to informed traders are 0.35 × E[(V − 21 − d)+]. Expected profit per side comes out to about +0.17 at d = 4, +0.21 at d = 5 and +0.21 at d = 6, so the best half-spread is around 5 to 6.

{"bid": 15.6, "ask": 26.4}

Fair value is 21: each card averages 7, and three cards give 3 × 7 = 21. The standard deviation of the sum is about 6.35.

I traded off two effects to set the spread. Noise traders pay me the half-spread when they trade. Informed traders take money from me whenever the true sum lies outside my quotes. The expected profit per quote is roughly flat and highest for a half-spread of about 6 to 7, so I'm using 6.5 on each side of 21.

{"bid": 14.5, "ask": 27.5}

Card 1 is a King (13), so the public fair value is about 26.8 (13 plus two unseen cards averaging about 6.88 each). The earlier buy at 27.5 and sell at 18.5 roughly cancel out, so I am not shifting from that.

The two unseen cards have a standard deviation of about 5.3, which makes adverse selection by informed traders a real risk. I am quoting about ±3.5 around fair value.

{"bid": 23.3, "ask": 30.3}

Card 1 is a 13, so the prior expected sum is about 13 + 2 × 6.88 ≈ 26.8. Then two buyers in a row took my asks at 30.3 and 31. That is evidence the value is high, which lifts my estimate of fair value to about 30–31.

I'm short 2, so I'll skew my quotes upward. That makes it more likely someone sells to me and less likely I sell more.

{"bid": 28.6, "ask": 33.4}

The expected sum of three cards is 21 (mean card value 7). The sum has a standard deviation of about 6.4. No trade at the wide quote of 14.5 / 27.5 tells us little, so fair value stays at about 21.

I'll tighten to a half-spread of about 4. That should attract noise flow while still giving some protection against informed traders.

{"bid": 17.0, "ask": 25.0}

Card 1 is a 0, so 14 cards remain, with values summing to 119. That gives a mean of 8.5 per card, so the expected settlement is about 17. The two unknown cards sum to about 17 ± 7.7.

Half the counterparties are informed, so I keep the quote wide around 17. That limits losses to informed traders while still catching some noise flow.

{"bid": 11.5, "ask": 22.5}

Two-card sum: each card averages 8.25, so the expected sum (fair value) is **16.5**, with a standard deviation of about **4.1**.

Informed and noise traders have about the same spread around fair value. So the half-spread is chosen to balance two things:
- what I earn from noise traders, roughly h·(1−Φ(h/σ)) per side;
- what I lose to informed traders.

Setting h/σ ≈ 1.2 maximises the expected profit per side, which gives a half-spread of about 5.

{"bid": 11.5, "ask": 21.5}

Fair value is about 23.4. Each card averages 7.8, so three cards sum to 3 × 7.8 = 23.4. The standard deviation of the sum is about 7.2.

The last quote (18.4 / 28.4) got no trade. That hints slightly that the value sits near the middle, so fair value stays around 23.4. I'll tighten the spread a little and stay symmetric around 23.4. That leaves enough width to protect against informed traders while still drawing some noise-trader flow.

{"bid": 19.5, "ask": 27.3}

Fair value is about 18. The deck averages 76/13 ≈ 5.85 per card, so three cards give 17.5. The first quote not trading nudges this up slightly, because it makes the very low sums a bit less likely.

The sum is likely to be either low (around 7–8, about 29% of the time) or high (18 or more). I'm quoting wide around fair value to protect against informed traders.

{"bid": 12, "ask": 24}

Expected value per card is 53/13 ≈ 4.08, so three cards are expected to sum to about 12.2. The standard deviation of the sum is about 6, and the noise traders' standard deviation is 8.9.

To protect against informed traders, I quote roughly ±4 around fair value.

{"bid": 8.4, "ask": 16.0}

The prior fair value is 12.25 (3.5 × 3.5), and the product is spread widely (standard deviation about 8.9). About 14/36 of outcomes are 6 or below and 10/36 are 18 or above. That means informed traders are likely to hit either side unless the spread stays wide.

The no-trade at 6.2/18.4 tells us very little, so the centre stays near 12.25. I'll narrow the quote slightly to catch more noise flow while still guarding against informed traders.

{"bid": 8.0, "ask": 16.6}

Card 1 is a 15, which is the highest card in the deck, so the contract will settle at exactly 15. Informed traders value it at 15, and noise traders value it at about N(15, 2.7).

For each side, I want the edge times the chance a noise trader trades. For a normal distribution, that product peaks with an edge of about 0.75σ, which is roughly 2. So I quote about 2 either side of 15. I'm short 2, so I can still sell one more unit, and a sale at 17 would also be profitable.

{"bid": 13.0, "ask": 17.0}

Fair value is 43.4. The coin's chance of heads averages 0.62 (0.4 × 0.5 + 0.6 × 0.7), so expected heads over 7 flips are 4.34, and each head is worth 10.

The settlement value is quite uncertain, with a standard deviation of about 14. An informed trader therefore often knows a value far from 43.4, so I need a wide spread to avoid losing to them. My last quote of 36 / 51 got no trade, so I'll tighten slightly to 37 / 50 to attract noise traders.

{"bid": 37, "ask": 50}

Given flip 1 = heads, the coin is more likely to be biased. The odds of it being the fair coin are now 0.625 against 0.375. That makes the expected number of heads 1 + 5×0.575 = 3.875, a value of about 19.4.

The buy at 24.9 in stage 1 also suggests an informed trader expecting at least 5 heads. Including that pushes my fair value estimate to about 22–23. I'm short 1 and will keep a wide spread around that level to protect against informed flow.

{"bid": 18.5, "ask": 28.0}

Given flip 1 = 0, the coin is more likely the 0.4 coin: about 0.818 versus 0.182 for the 0.8 coin. That makes the expected heads probability per flip about 0.473. With 5 flips left and none counted yet, fair value is about 5 × 0.473 ≈ 2.36.

The earlier buy at 4.3 hints slightly at higher value, so I use about 2.45. I'm short 1, so I lean my quotes a little upward. I keep the spread wide because informed traders know the outcome.

{"bid": 1.7, "ask": 3.3}

Given two red draws, the posterior on the number of red balls is proportional to R(R−1). That puts the fair value at 8 with a standard deviation of about 1.9.

The quote centres on 8. The spread is wide enough to cover the informed traders. They hurt the ask only when R = 10, about 27% likely. They hurt the bid only when R ≤ 6, about 21% likely. Even so, noise traders (standard deviation 2.4) will still reach both sides.

{"bid": 6.7, "ask": 9.3}

With two reds drawn, my posterior on the red count is weighted toward high values. Bayes from a uniform prior gives an expected value of about 7.25. The sale at my 3.8 bid on quote 7 pulls that down somewhat, to about 6.6.

I am long 3, so I can only post an ask. An ask at 9.5 or above can never be hit by an informed trader, because the most red balls possible is 9. Noise traders still buy above that level often enough to make a sale worth having. The expected profit from the sale (chance of a noise buy times the margin over fair value) peaks at an ask of about 10.

{"bid": null, "ask": 10.0}

The contract settles at 10 × the red balls left among the 9 undrawn. That count starts at a mean of 2.7, so a value of about 27. The last quote's no-trade at 1/13 matters: an informed trader would have bought at 13 if the value were 20 or more. Updating on that, the most likely outcome is 1 red ball (about 50%), and fair value drops to roughly 16–17.

I'm long 2, so I'll lean the quote slightly low to encourage selling while protecting against informed traders. The bid stays below 10, so an informed seller with value 10 won't hit it.

{"bid": 8, "ask": 19}

Draws 1 and 2 were both blue. Each ball was painted independently, so those draws tell us nothing about the other 9 balls. Expected reds among those 9 is 9 × 0.3 = 2.7, so fair value is about 27. The standard deviation is about 10 × √(9 × 0.21) ≈ 13.7.

The buyer at 19 last quote hints slightly upward, so I put fair value near 27–28. I'm long 1, so I shade both sides down a little to lean toward selling. The spread is wide to protect against informed traders.

{"bid": 21, "ask": 33}

Three draws so far are all blue, so the 8 unseen balls hold Bin(8, 0.3) reds. On the draws alone that is about 2.4 reds, worth 24. The trade history changes this a lot, though. The repeated sells at 20, 16, 21 and 14 and the no-trade at 1/13 all fit an informed trader with a value around 10. My rough Bayesian update puts most of the weight on 1 remaining red, so I estimate fair value at about 10–11.

I'm long 3 above that value, so I want to sell. An ask of 13 sits above my estimate of fair value but is still close enough to draw noise buyers.

{"bid": null, "ask": 13}

Given draw 1 is red, the other 9 balls are each still independently red with probability 0.6. So the expected red count is 1 + 9 × 0.6 = 6.4, a fair value of **64**. The uncertainty is about 10 × √(9 × 0.24) ≈ 14.7.

I am flat and 20% of counterparties are informed, so I quote symmetrically around 64 with a half-spread of about 9. That should catch a fair share of noise flow while staying clear of the informed traders' typical edge.

{"bid": 55, "ask": 73}

Both draws were red, so the remaining 8 balls are independent with P(red) = 0.6. That gives an expected red count of 2 + 4.8 = 6.8, so the prior value is about 68, with a standard deviation of about 14.

The trade at quote 3 (they sold at 55, when the public fair value was about 64) is evidence the value is lower. Updating on it brings the fair value down to about 64.5–65. I'm long 1, so I skew my quotes slightly down around 65. I keep the spread wide enough to cover the risk of trading with an informed counterparty.

